    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 10827.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 755,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to understand water is to understand the very foundation of life as we know it. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Three States)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 2. Unique Characteristics\nWater has several bizarre and remarkable scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of its chemical structure, it easily bonds with and carries minerals, nutrients, and chemicals. This is why rivers and oceans are salty, and how our bodies transport nutrients.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life, and life on Earth likely wouldn't exist.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how plants draw water up from their roots to their leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Earth is the \"Blue Planet\":** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite this vast amount, **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than **1%** of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater for human use.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is endlessly recycled on Earth through evaporation, condensation (clouds), precipitation (rain/snow), and collection. The water you drink today contains the exact same molecules that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, and the lungs are about 83%. We can survive for weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n*   **Civilization:** Throughout history, human civilizations have always risen near sources of water (rivers, coasts, lakes). It is essential for agriculture, sanitation, industry, and energy production (hydroelectric power).\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with complex properties that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
